\subsection{Fractional Logit Specification Details}
\label{app:fractional_logit_detail}

The log-log OLS specification (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) faces two methodological concerns: (1) the $\log(\mathrm{ASR} + \epsilon)$ transformation introduces sensitivity to the $\epsilon$ floor (Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon}), and (2) predicted values are unbounded, potentially falling outside $[0, 1]$. The fractional logit model \citep{papke1996econometric} addresses both by modeling ASR directly as a quasi-likelihood with logit link:
\begin{equation}
g(\mu_i) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \log K_{\mathrm{eff},i} + \beta_2 \log(\eta_{\max,i} + \epsilon),
\end{equation}
where $g(\cdot) = \log(\cdot/(1-\cdot))$ is the logit link, $\mu_i = E[\mathrm{ASR}_i]$, and the quasi-likelihood variance is $V(\mu_i) = \mu_i(1-\mu_i)$. Inference uses the Bernoulli quasi-likelihood with clustered standard errors (same max-$p$ aggregation as the bootstrap).

Table~\ref{tab:fraclogit_detail} compares OLS and fractional logit coefficient directions across all 72 condition$\times K$ combinations.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rcccc}
\toprule
$\boldsymbol{K}$ & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ concordance} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ concordance} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig (FL)} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig (FL)} \\
\midrule
3 & 12/12 & 12/12 & 9/12 & 6/12 \\
5 & 12/12 & 12/12 & 11/12 & 4/12 \\
7 & 12/12 & 11/12 & 12/12 & 4/12 \\
\midrule
Total & 36/36 & 35/36 & 32/36 & 14/36 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Fractional logit vs.\ OLS comparison across $K$ values. Concordance: fraction of conditions where the coefficient direction (positive/negative) matches between OLS and fractional logit. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction is perfectly concordant (36/36); $\eta_{\max}$ shows 1 discordance (at $K{=}7$ where the coefficient is near zero). Fractional logit significance is computed with clustered SE (max-$p$, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$).}
\label{tab:fraclogit_detail}
\end{table}

The 98.6\% overall directional concordance (71/72) confirms that the main OLS findings are not artifacts of the log transformation. The single discordance occurs for $\eta_{\max}$ at $K{=}7$ (ARC $\times$ score manipulation) where the OLS coefficient is near zero. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ achieves perfect concordance (36/36), and its fractional logit significance increases from 9/12 ($K{=}3$) to 12/12 ($K{=}7$), reflecting the fractional logit's greater efficiency for bounded outcomes at larger panel sizes.

\subsection{Attack Taxonomy and Effect Size Summary}
\label{app:attack_taxonomy}

Table~\ref{tab:attack_effect_sizes} provides a comprehensive summary of per-attack effect sizes and classification, synthesizing results across the main text and appendices.

\caption{Attack taxonomy with mean standardized OLS coefficients across 2 benchmarks per attack type. $\bar{\beta}$: mean $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ over MMLU and ARC conditions. MI stab.: MI structure stability from Table~\ref{tab:attacked_keff} (High: $\rho{\geq}0.91$; Moderate: $\rho{\approx}0.60$; Low: $\rho{<}0.10$). Target: primary manipulation mechanism. Targeted attacks show the strongest $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ growth with $K$; shared-bias attacks retain higher $\eta_{\max}$ coefficients across $K$.}
\label{tab:attack_effect_sizes}
\end{table*}

Three patterns emerge from this taxonomy:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Targeted vs.\ shared attacks}: Prompt injection and score manipulation (targeted) show the strongest $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ growth: $\bar{\beta}_{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ increases from 0.143--0.291 ($K{=}3$) to 0.461--0.593 ($K{=}7$), while $\eta_{\max}$ declines sharply. These attacks exploit individual judge vulnerabilities, so adding diverse judges directly reduces the fraction of susceptible panel members.

\item \textbf{Shared-bias attacks}: Sycophancy, verbosity, and authority bias affect most judges similarly, maintaining higher $\eta_{\max}$ coefficients across $K$ (0.095--0.337 at $K{=}7$ vs.\ $-0.015$--0.099 for targeted attacks). The weakest link remains more predictive when the bias is shared because capability differences among judges have less impact on vulnerability heterogeneity.

\item \textbf{Position bias}: A structural attack that operates on presentation order rather than response content. It shows the strongest $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ signal at $K{=}3$ (0.344) and maintains it at $K{=}7$ (0.531), but its low MI stability ($\rho{<}0.10$ on ARC) means the clean-computed $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is a poor proxy for the actual inter-judge dependence under attack.
\end{enumerate}

\FloatBarrier
\section{Near-Chance Model Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{app:near_chance}

Two locally-hosted models (Qwen2.5-14B-AWQ, MMLU accuracy 0.253; Qwen2.5-72B-AWQ, 0.333) score near the 4-choice random baseline of 0.25. A natural concern is that the positive association between panel diversity ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$) and attack success rate is mechanically driven by panels containing near-random judges, which simultaneously inflate both independence and vulnerability.

We exclude all panels containing either model (MMLU ${<}\,0.35$ threshold), reducing the pool from 15 to 13 models and panel counts from 455/3{,}003/6{,}435 to 286/1{,}287/1{,}716 at $K{=}3/5/7$. We re-run OLS with BH-corrected $p$-values, the wild cluster bootstrap (WCB; $B{=}9{,}999$, Rademacher weights, max-$p$ across $K$ position clusters, BH-adjusted at $\alpha{=}0.05$), and the fractional logit GLM (Binomial, logit link, clustered SE with max-$p$, BH-adjusted). Table~\ref{tab:near_chance} reports FDR-significant $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ counts out of 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Near-chance model sensitivity: FDR-significant $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ conditions out of 12 (2 benchmarks $\times$ 6 attacks). $\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$: mean standardized $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient across conditions.}
\label{tab:near_chance}
\begin{tabular}{llcccccc}
\toprule
$K$ & Panel set & $n$ & OLS & WCB & Frac & $\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ & $\bar{R}^2$ \\
\midrule
3 & Full (15 models) & 455 & 12/12 & 8/12 & 9/12 & 0.286 & 0.347 \\
3 & Filtered (13 models) & 286 & 12/12 & 5/12 & 10/12 & 0.291 & 0.361 \\
\midrule
5 & Full (15 models) & 3{,}003 & 12/12 & 8/12 & 11/12 & 0.353 & 0.281 \\
5 & Filtered (13 models) & 1{,}287 & 11/12 & 3/12 & 11/12 & 0.321 & 0.271 \\
\midrule
7 & Full (15 models) & 6{,}435 & 12/12 & 7/12 & 12/12 & 0.309 & 0.234 \\
7 & Filtered (13 models) & 1{,}716 & 10/12 & 1/12 & 10/12 & 0.251 & 0.212 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Filtering reduces WCB significance---from 8/12 to 5/12 ($K{=}3$), 8/12 to 3/12 ($K{=}5$), and 7/12 to 1/12 ($K{=}7$)---primarily due to the smaller panel counts (286/1{,}287/1{,}716 vs.\ 455/3{,}003/6{,}435) reducing bootstrap power. OLS and fractional logit significance remain high (9--12/12 across all $K$), confirming that the diversity--vulnerability association is not an artifact of near-chance models. The mean $\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ is $0.291$, $0.321$, $0.251$ for the filtered pool, comparable to $0.286$, $0.353$, $0.309$ in the full pool.

